      <p class="text-sm text-gray-500">All entry through 64GB tiers share the same platform: AMD EPYC, NVMe SSD, KVM, cPanel/WHM, CloudLinux, 99.99% SLA. Source: tmdhosting.com/vps-hosting.html (August 2026).</p>

      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-white mt-10 mb-4">Hardware & Software Stack</h2>
      <p>The platform is consistent across every plan — there is no "good-better-best" hardware spectrum, only a capacity one. That makes TMDHosting predictable in a way many providers are not.</p>

      <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-white mt-6 mb-3">CPU & Memory</h3>
      <p>TMDHosting uses AMD EPYC on every shared plan and reserves the dedicated CPU cores per plan tier. RAM is <strong>100% dedicated</strong> (not burstable) — the 4GB plan gives you 4GB, not "4GB burstable to 8GB." That matters for memory-hungry stacks (Node.js, Elasticsearch, Meilisearch) where shared-burstable models inflate your Tailwind-style "is it working?" panics.</p>

      <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-white mt-6 mb-3">Storage</h3>
      <p>All plans ship with <strong>NVMe SSD</strong> storage. There is no slow-tier fall-back — even the $69/mo entry has NVMe. The 16GB tier hits the sweet spot of 300GB NVMe at $169/mo.</p>

      <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-white mt-6 mb-3">Web Stack</h3>
      <p>CloudLinux isolates each account on shared resources; Apache HTTP/2 + HTTP/3 + QUIC are enabled by default. Plans at 16GB RAM and above add <strong>Litespeed</strong> for higher-request-per-second throughput under WordPress, WooCommerce, and Magento workloads. PHP 5.6 through 8.5 are selectable in the MultiPHP manager; MariaDB 11.4 is the default database.</p>

      <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-white mt-6 mb-3">Security (Bundled, Not Add-On)</h3>
      <p>Security is included on every tier — that's the practical upside of the all-included pricing model. You get auto-renewing Auto SSL, a Web Application Firewall (WAF), Monarx malware scanner, daily backups, and 24/7/365 server monitoring without separate subscriptions.</p>

      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-white mt-10 mb-4">Data Center Footprint (8+ Global Locations)</h2>
      <p>TMDHosting operates <strong>eight or more global data centers</strong> across five continents, verified from their <a href="https://www.tmdhosting.com/data-centers.html" class="text-orange-400 hover:underline">data-centers.html</a> page on 2026-08-18:</p>
      <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-2 text-gray-300">
        <li><strong>United States:</strong> Chicago, IL</li>
        <li><strong>United Kingdom:</strong> London</li>
        <li><strong>Netherlands:</strong> Amsterdam</li>
        <li><strong>Singapore:</strong> (Southeast Asia hub)</li>
        <li><strong>Australia:</strong> Sydney</li>
        <li><strong>Japan:</strong> Tokyo</li>
        <li><strong>South Africa:</strong> Johannesburg</li>
        <li><strong>India:</strong> Mumbai</li>
      </ul>
      <p>Each data center uses Juniper + Cisco + Dell edge routers aggregated through Cisco and Juniper switches. TMDHosting publishes a 99.99% uptime guarantee backed by a credit SLA.</p>

      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-white mt-10 mb-4">Who TMDHosting Is Best For</h2>
      <p>Three profiles fit TMDHosting well: traditional cPanel users, small-business managed-hosting refugees, and agencies reselling hosting.</p>

      <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-white mt-6 mb-3">1. cPanel-Committed Users</h3>
      <p>If your stack is WordPress + WooCommerce + a stack of Softaculous one-click apps and you're not ready to learn a new control panel, cPanel-on-TMDHosting keeps your operational tooling consistent. Compared to Liquid Web (which charges separately for cPanel on certain plans), TMDHosting's cPanel inclusion is straightforward.</p>

      <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-white mt-6 mb-3">2. Small Businesses Looking for "Set It and Forget It" Managed Linux</h3>
      <p>The all-included model (cPanel + CloudLinux + Auto SSL + WAF + daily backups + monitoring) means the small-business owner is not paying separate subscriptions for each service. Compare to bare-budget hosts where you negotiate, install, and patch everything yourself.</p>

      <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-white mt-6 mb-3">3. Agencies Reselling Hosting</h3>
      <p>cPanel + WHM gives agencies a familiar multi-account management interface. The 24/7 human support deflects Level-1 tickets that would otherwise eat into an agency's billable hours.</p>

      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-white mt-10 mb-4">Where TMDHosting Falls Short</h2>
      <p>The same specialization that makes TMDHosting good for cPanel users becomes a ceiling for power users.</p>

      <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-white mt-6 mb-3">No GPU Instances</h3>
      <p>Unlike <a href="/vps/vultr" class="text-orange-400 hover:underline">Vultr</a>, <a href="/vps/linode" class="text-orange-400 hover:underline">Linode (Akamai)</a>, or <a href="/vps/cherryservers" class="text-orange-400 hover:underline">Cherry Servers</a>, TMDHosting does not offer GPU compute tiers. If your workload is fine-tuning, inference, or AI serving, look elsewhere.</p>

      <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-white mt-6 mb-3">No Custom ISO / Bare-Metal Reclaim</h3>
      <p>For users who want to upload custom ISOs (FreeBSD, OpenBSD, custom Linux distros) or run specialized kernels, TMDHosting's managed stack is the wrong tool. <a href="/vps/cherryservers" class="text-orange-400 hover:underline">Cherry Servers</a> or <a href="/vps/ovhcloud" class="text-orange-400 hover:underline">OVHcloud</a> are better fits.</p>

      <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-white mt-6 mb-3">Higher Entry Price Than Budget Hosts</h3>
      <p>$69/mo for the entry tier is roughly 35× the entry price of <a href="/vps/racknerd" class="text-orange-400 hover:underline">RackNerd</a> ($1.99/mo) and 17× the entry of <a href="/vps/hosthatch" class="text-orange-400 hover:underline">HostHatch</a>. For pure-budget Linux VPS where you manage yourself, the headroom is wrong.</p>

      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-white mt-10 mb-4">Comparison: TMDHosting vs Other Managed VPS Providers on VPSTier</h2>
      <p>We've reviewed 60+ VPS providers on vpstier.com. Here's how TMDHosting stacks up to the closest comparables.</p>

      <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-white mt-6 mb-3">TMDHosting vs Liquid Web</h3>
      <p><a href="/vps/liquidweb" class="text-orange-400 hover:underline">Liquid Web</a> starts at $5/mo for self-managed VPS; TMDHosting starts at $69/mo for fully managed cPanel VPS. Liquid Web charges separately for cPanel (typically $15-30/mo add-on) and management (varies by tier). TMDHosting's flat $69/mo for fully managed cPanel is straightforward but less flexible — you don't get the cheap self-managed floor if you're comfortable with command-line administration.</p>

      <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-white mt-6 mb-3">TMDHosting vs Atlantic.Net</h3>
      <p><a href="/vps/atlantic-net" class="text-orange-400 hover:underline">Atlantic.Net</a> offers $8/mo entry VPS with 100% uptime SLA. Like Liquid Web, Atlantic.Net's entry tier is non-managed — you admin everything yourself. TMDHosting's value is in the management layer, not the hardware price per dollar.</p>

      <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-white mt-6 mb-3">TMDHosting vs Cloudways</h3>
      <p><a href="/vps/cloudways" class="text-orange-400 hover:underline">Cloudways</a> is managed cloud, but uses a custom panel (not cPanel) and runs on top of AWS, Google Cloud, DigitalOcean, Linode, or Vultr. TMDHosting is cPanel-only and runs on its own infrastructure. Choose TMDHosting if you want cPanel; choose Cloudways if you want to be on a specific hyperscaler without learning AWS console.</p>

      <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-white mt-6 mb-3">TMDHosting vs HostArmada</h3>
      <p><a href="/vps/hostarmada" class="text-orange-400 hover:underline">HostArmada</a> is newer and cheaper at the entry tier but lacks TMDHosting's 18+ year track record and the AMD EPYC + NVMe across all tiers consistency.</p>

      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-white mt-10 mb-4">FAQ: TMDHosting VPS in 2026</h2>

      <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-white mt-6 mb-3">What is the cheapest TMDHosting VPS plan?</h3>
      <p>The cheapest managed VPS plan is <strong>Managed cPanel 4GB at $69/month paid monthly</strong>. It includes 2 vCPU (AMD EPYC), 4GB dedicated RAM, 100GB NVMe SSD, and 1TB monthly bandwidth. cPanel/WHM, CloudLinux, daily backups, SSL, and WAF are all bundled.</p>

      <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-white mt-6 mb-3">Does TMDHosting include cPanel/WHM in the price?</h3>
      <p>Yes. TMDHosting bundles a cPanel/WHM license with every managed VPS plan — no separate cPanel license fee. CloudLinux, Softaculous (one-click installs), Apache HTTP/2 + HTTP/3, Litespeed (on 16GB+ tiers), and 24/7/365 human monitoring are also bundled.</p>

      <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-white mt-6 mb-3">Where are TMDHosting's data centers?</h3>
      <p>TMDHosting operates 8+ global data centers: Chicago (US), London (UK), Amsterdam (NL), Singapore, Sydney, Tokyo, Johannesburg, and Mumbai. Each location uses Juniper + Cisco + Dell edge routers with a 99.99% uptime guarantee.</p>

      <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-white mt-6 mb-3">What is TMDHosting's hardware stack?</h3>
      <p>AMD EPYC CPUs on every shared plan, NVMe SSD storage (no SSD-only fallback tier), KVM virtualization, 100% dedicated RAM per plan tier, and CloudLinux for shared-resource isolation.</p>

      <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-white mt-6 mb-3">How does TMDHosting compare to Liquid Web, Atlantic.Net, or Cloudways?</h3>
      <p>TMDHosting's $69/mo entry is higher than Liquid Web's $5/mo non-managed or Atlantic.Net's $8/mo non-managed, but it bundles cPanel/WHM, CloudLinux, daily backups, SSL, and WAF into the flat price. Liquid Web and Atlantic.Net charge separately for management. Cloudways is managed multi-cloud (not cPanel). TMDHosting fits the user who wants cPanel + managed service in one flat monthly bill.</p>

      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-white mt-10 mb-4">Final Verdict</h2>
      <p>TMDHosting's 2026 line-up is what an established cPanel-managed Linux VPS should look like in 2026: AMD EPYC + NVMe on every tier, 8+ global data centers, CloudLinux + Litespeed where it counts, and an SLA-backed 99.99% uptime guarantee. It is not the cheapest — at $69/mo entry it sits well above budget hosts — but it is one of the more honest "all-included" managed offerings on the market.</p>

      <p>If you want a cPanel-friendly, managed Linux VPS with predictable monthly pricing and 19 years of operating history, TMDHosting is a defensible choice. For pure-budget Linux VPS, see our <a href="/vps/racknerd" class="text-orange-400 hover:underline">RackNerd review</a>. For managed cloud without cPanel, see <a href="/vps/cloudways" class="text-orange-400 hover:underline">Cloudways</a>.</p>

